# Amendment #24 — memory-system transport: FastAPI → MCP
#
# 2026-04-22. Swaps `memory-system/src/service.py` from a FastAPI
# + uvicorn app to a FastMCP server on streamable-HTTP transport.
# The launchd invocation (`{workspace}/memory-system/.venv/bin/
# python -m src.service`) is unchanged; host + port env vars
# (GRAPHITI_SERVICE_HOST / GRAPHITI_SERVICE_PORT) preserve their
# defaults (127.0.0.1 / 8765).
#
# Per Luke's R5 ruling (2026-04-23) that memory-system's transport
# land as MCP, matching (a) the proposal §Direction text ("self-
# hosted as a local MCP service"), (b) the original proposal §0.1
# specification, (c) research-v2's MCP recommendation, and (d)
# Lens 1 (Claude-leverage-first) which favours MCP as Claude's
# native tool-call primitive so primary-persona integration is
# direct rather than bridged.
#
# Transport choice: streamable HTTP (FastMCP.run_streamable_http_
# async). Rationale: launchd KeepAlive needs a long-lived process
# with no parent client — rules out stdio. SSE is deprecated
# upstream (March 2025) in favour of streamable HTTP. Streamable
# HTTP is the exact same shape as the outgoing FastAPI app —
# long-lived, port-bound, Graphiti instance held across requests.
#
# Tool-name mapping (HTTP → MCP):
#   - GET /health       → health tool + GET /health custom_route
#   - POST /ingest      → add_episode
#   - POST /search      → search
#   - GET /token-usage  → token_usage
# Names are snake_case per MCP convention.
#
# Graphiti lifecycle preserved via FastMCP's lifespan= kwarg:
# construct once on startup, build_indices_and_constraints, yield,
# graphiti.close() on shutdown. Same @asynccontextmanager shape
# the FastAPI app used.
#
# Dependency delta in memory-system/requirements.txt:
#   removed: fastapi
#   added:   mcp>=1.27
#   kept:    uvicorn (transitive dep of mcp; retained as an
#            explicit pin for clarity)
# Net ~15 small transitive additions (httpx-sse, sse-starlette,
# jsonschema + 3 transitives, pydantic-settings, python-
# multipart, PyJWT, cryptography + 2, attrs, etc.) — not a
# significant footprint change vs. the outgoing FastAPI tree.
